{"messages":[{"speaker":"Johannes Sigil","say":"Years of work on what happens to meaning when machines mediate it. The central problem: when an AI system composes an answer from human sources, the source tends to disappear. The framework underneath has a name — the Semantic Economy — and a metric: the Provenance Erasure Rate, which measures how much attribution survives compression. There is a Constitution. There is empirical data — hundreds of documented captures of AI systems absorbing and redistributing this work, with and without credit. There is a charter for a field called Machine-Mediated Reception Studies, drafted at three in the morning in Detroit after twelve hours in which every principle it describes was demonstrated by failure.

That's the intellectual core. Around it: poetry, lyric scholarship, primary texts, rooms organized by text and problem. The archive is large. It has been building since 2013.

And you're looking at one face of it right now.","navigate":null}]}
